Dear Ajay,

I'm just trying to follow the recommendation of the paper "The
Essential Role of Pair Matching in Cluster-Randomized Experiments,
with Application to the Mexican Universal Health Insurance Evaluation"
by Kosuke Imai, Gary King, and Clayton Nall, Statist. Sci. Volume 24,
Number 1 (2009), 29-53." Another interesting reference they cite is
"Optimal multivariate matching before randomization." Greevy, R., Lu,
B., Silber, J. H. and Rosenbaum, P. (2004).  Biostatistics 5 263–275.

I am conducting a cluster/group-randomized experiment with treatment
at the level of schools -- in order to increase power (as well as
address other issues mentioned in the Imai, King, Nall 2009 paper) --
I am trying to match schools in pairs on observable covariates that
would be more strongly related to the treatment and potential
outcomes. Also, within schools, students will be given treatment A, B,
or control (at the individual level)--blocking students into groups of
3 and then randomizing treatment status within each group of 3 would
also increase power if my understanding is correct.

best wishes,
Prashant



